# Phase 1 module contract — how the room modules plug together

> **Describes the first build (2026-09-25), not yet the current design.**
> The planning document has since adopted "a room is a contact" (section
> 15, now sections 1–4), and issues 101–106 and 103a were rewritten for it.
> This contract is
> rewritten together with the code, as issue 101 says; until then, read the
> issues for what the modules are meant to offer.

Phase 1 ("Rooms on the home server") is built as five modules and one demo,
each by its own builder, each in its own folder.  This document is the plug
shape they share, so each can be built before its neighbours exist.  It is
the reference the issue files 101–106 point to.

If a module needs to change the shape described here, it writes the change
into this document and posts a note on the builders' board (below) — it does
not quietly change a neighbour's file.

## Where each module lives

| issue | module folder | reading-order indexes | owns |
|---|---|---|---|
| 101 | `src/room-folders/` | 001–019 | the home folder, config, contacts, room folders, the change bell |
| 102 | `src/room-tree/` | 020–039 | parents and children, the `where` file, moving rooms, what a reader learns about parents |
| 103 | `src/recipients/` | 040–059 | recipient lists, removal splitting a room, rooms made on first arrival |
| 104 | `src/people-links/` | 060–079 | the `people/` folder of links |
| 105 | `src/message-log/` | 080–099 | the message log, message ids, ordering, duplicate refusal, edits |
| 106 | `src/phase-1-demo/`, `scripts/`, `issues/completed/demos/` | 100–119 | the options tally and the phase 1 demo |

Every source file's name starts with its reading-order index (for example
`src/room-folders/002-room-folders.lua`).  Tests live in each module's own
`tests/` folder.  Each source file has a `<file>.info.md` beside it listing
its outward-facing functions with their inputs and outputs.

## Loading one module from another

`src/room-folders/001-load-path.lua` returns a function; called with the
project folder, it adds every `src/<module>/` folder to Lua's search path.
After that, a module is loaded by its file name without `.lua`:

    dofile(DIR .. "/src/room-folders/001-load-path.lua")(DIR)
    local rooms = require("002-room-folders")

## The home folder (rao-home)

    rao-home/
      config        key = value lines (rmail's config format)
      contacts      name.field = value lines (rmail's contacts format)
      rooms/<room id>/{name, parent, recipients, where, log}
      people/<contact>/<link name> -> ../../rooms/<room id>

A **home** value passed between modules is a table `{ dir = <string, absolute
path of rao-home> }`.

A **room id** is a string of 32 lower-case hexadecimal characters, read from
`/dev/urandom`.  "No parent" is the empty string `""`, never nil.

## The change bell (owned by 101)

Modules keep derived files (`where`, `people/` links) up to date by listening
for changes instead of calling each other directly.

- `rooms.on_change(listener)` — registers `listener(home, room_id, what)`.
- `rooms.notify(home, room_id, what)` — rings the bell; 101 rings it for its
  own writes, 105 rings it with `"log"` after filing a message.
- `what` is one of: `"created"`, `"name"`, `"parent"`, `"recipients"`,
  `"log"`, `"removed-room"`, `"split"` (103 rings it on the old id once the
  room is a read-only split record).  Any other value is an error.
- A listener that raises an error stops the ring, and the error reaches
  whoever made the change (no summary is left silently stale).

Listeners are registered when a module is loaded; loading 102 keeps `where`
current, loading 104 keeps `people/` current.

## The functions each module offers

Types: `string`, `integer` (whole number), `list` (Lua array), `bool`.

### 101 room-folders — `require("002-room-folders")`

| function | takes | gives |
|---|---|---|
| `open_home(dir)` | string | home; creates the layout if absent |
| `read_config(home)` | home | table string→string |
| `set_config(home, key, value)` | home, string, string | — |
| `config_value(home, key)` | home, string | string; **error** if the key is absent |
| `read_contacts(home)` | home | table name→{field→string} |
| `new_room_id()` | — | room id |
| `create_room(home, spec)` | spec `{name=string, parent_id=string, recipients=list of string, room_id=string or absent}` | room id |
| `read_room(home, id)` | home, room id | `{id, name, parent_id, recipients=list}` |
| `write_name / write_parent(home, id, value)` | | — (rings the bell) |
| `write_recipients(home, id, list)` | | — (rings the bell) |
| `list_rooms(home)` | home | sorted list of room ids |
| `room_exists(home, id)` | | bool |
| `room_dir(home, id)` / `log_path(home, id)` | | string |
| `on_change(listener)` / `notify(home, id, what)` | | — |
| `is_room_id(value)` | any | bool (extra) |
| `listener_count()` | — | integer (extra; for tests and the demo) |

101's rules the others can rely on: `create_room` builds the room in a
hidden `rooms/.new-<id>/` — `name`, `parent`, `recipients` and an **empty
`log`** — and renames it into place, then rings only `"created"`; no room
is ever visible without its log, and the log is 105's from then on.
Every file write is write-to-temporary-then-rename.  A
parent that would put a room inside itself is refused by `create_room`
and `write_parent`.  Recipient names must match rmail's contact-name shape
(`[A-Za-z0-9_-]+`), may not repeat, may not be `"me"`, and are not checked
against `contacts`; an empty list is allowed.  `read_room` and every
`write_*` error on a room that does not exist.  `list_rooms` skips hidden
entries and errors on any other entry in `rooms/` that is not a room id.
`read_contacts` keeps indexed fields as written (`c["ip[2]"]`) and strips
double quotes from values.

### 102 room-tree — `require("021-room-tree")`

| function | gives |
|---|---|
| `children_of(home, id)` | list of room ids |
| `ancestors(home, id)` | list of room ids, top first, ending with `id` |
| `path_of_names(home, id)` | string such as `"home/ritz/programming"` |
| `write_where(home, id)` | rewrites that room's `where` and every room below it |
| `move_room(home, id, new_parent_id)` | refuses a move that would put a room inside itself |
| `parent_to_disclose(home, id, contact, mode)` | parent id or `""` (open question 3 variants) |
| `visible_forest(received)` | a reader's trees, from a list of `{room_id, name, disclosed_parent_id}`: a list of top nodes `{room_id, name, disclosed_parent_id, parent_unseen=bool, children=list of nodes}`, sorted by name then id |
| `write_all_where(home)` | rewrites `where` for every room (a home made before 102 was loaded) |
| `read_where(home, id)` | `{folder=string, names=string}` from the room's `where` |
| `disclosure_mode(home)` | the config value of `tree.parent-disclosure` (errors when absent or unknown) |
| `what_contact_receives(home, contact, mode)` | the `received` list that contact would hold if every room they are in spoke now |
| `forest_paths(forest)` | every room of a forest as a path of names, in drawing order |

`where` is two lines, `folder = <room folder>` and `names = <path of
names>`.  `ancestors` follows 101's `rooms.unknown-parent`: under `keep` a
room whose parent is not held here is a top (so `ancestors(id)[1]` may name
an unheld parent in its own `parent` file); under `refuse` it is an error.
Under `rooms.slash-in-name = allow`, a `/` inside a name is written `\/`
(and `\` as `\\`) in paths; `022-where-file`'s `split_path` reverses it.
The bell listener rewrites `where` on `created`, `name`, `parent` and
ignores the other kinds.  Drawing a forest as text is `024-forest-text`
(`lines(forest)`, `text(forest)`).

### 103 recipients — `require("041-recipients")`

| function | gives |
|---|---|
| `add(home, id, contact)` / `remove(home, id, contact)` | — |
| `split_on_removal(home, id, remover, mode)` | the room id you now talk in; `""` when none (room of two under `recipients.split-alone = record-only`) |
| `room_from_arrival(home, id, sender, mode, carried)` | room id, and a list of carried names left out because they are not contacts (empty outside `adder-list`).  `carried` is `{recipients = list of string}` in the **reader's** contact names, the reader written `"me"`; only `adder-list` reads it |
| `live_room_for(home, id)` | the room id a message addressed to `id` should be filed in (follows fork records), or `""` when there is none here and the room must be made on arrival |
| `configured_split_shape(home)` / `configured_arrival_list(home)` | the config value for `recipients.split-shape` / `recipients.arrival-list` (errors when absent) |

103 keeps two marker files inside room folders (`key = value` lines):
`rooms/<id>/split` on a room that is now a read-only record of a split
(`into`, `remover`, `shape`), and `rooms/<id>/arrival` on a room made on
first arrival (`mode`, `sender`, repeatable `declined`).  103 listens for
`"log"` on the bell to grow heard-from rooms; it reads the log through
105's `read`.  Rooms whose parent was the split room are re-parented to
the new room first (each rings `"parent"`).  Then a fork split has rung
`"created"` for the new room (via `create_room`) and `"log"` (via
`copy_log`), and finally rings `"split"` on the old id once its marker is
written; a rekey split rings `"removed-room"` for the old id then
`"created"` for the new one; a record-only split rings `"split"` only.

### 104 people-links — `require("061-people-links")`

| function | gives |
|---|---|
| `top_rooms_for(home, contact)` | list of room ids |
| `rebuild_person(home, contact)` / `rebuild_all(home)` | — |
| `planned_links(home, contact)` | list of `{path, target, room_id, folder, name}` (strings) — what the folder should hold, without touching the disk |

Loading 104 registers a bell listener: `"created"`, `"name"`, `"parent"`,
`"recipients"`, `"removed-room"` and `"split"` rebuild all of `people/`;
`"log"` does nothing.  Every rebuild reads `people.link-collision`,
`people.own-devices` and `people.split-records`, so once 104 is loaded
those three keys must be set before any room is written.  A room whose
parent is not held here is read through 101's `rooms.unknown-parent`
(`keep`: it is a top), and a room name holding `/` through
`rooms.slash-in-name` and `people.slash-in-link-name` — both only when the
case occurs.  104 reads 103's `rooms/<id>/split` marker (never writes it).
Only links shaped `people/<c>/<name> -> ../../rooms/<id>` or
`people/<c>/<id>/<name> -> ../../../rooms/<id>` are ever removed.

### 105 message-log — `require("081-message-log")`

A **message** is `{id=string, author=string, written=integer, arrived=integer,
text=string}`; `author` is `"me"` or a contact name; times are whole seconds
since 1970.

| function | gives |
|---|---|
| `new_message_id()` | string |
| `append(home, room_id, message)` | `"filed"` or `"duplicate"` |
| `has_message(home, room_id, message_id)` | bool |
| `read(home, room_id)` | list of records in file order: each message as above plus `kind` (`"message"`, `"correction"`, `"deletion"`) and `target` (the id an edit changes; `""` for a message) — both always present |
| `read_ordered(home, room_id)` | messages only, edits applied, sorted by written time, then room id, then message id; each `{id, author, written, arrived, text, edited=bool, deleted=bool, room_id}` (a deleted message stays, text `""`, so its anchor resolves) |
| `copy_log(home, from_id, to_id)` | integer, records newly filed; each record re-filed through the duplicate check in the current shape (copying twice is safe); rings `"log"` once on `to_id` when anything was filed |
| `correct(home, room_id, correction)` | `{id (new), author, written, arrived, target, text}` → `"filed"`/`"duplicate"`; error when `log.edits` refuses (only the author may edit) |
| `delete(home, room_id, deletion)` | `{id (new), author, written, arrived, target}` → as `correct` |

105 needs these config keys set (absent is an error): `log.record-shape`,
`log.shape-mixing`, `log.duplicate-check`, `log.edits`, and
`log.edit-window-seconds` (a whole number, read only under `window`).
Message ids may hold only letters, digits, `-` and `_`; authors may not
hold tab, newline or CR.

## Choices the owner has not made yet

Where an open question in `notes/vision-planning.md` touches a module, the
module builds **every** variant, chosen by a key in `rao-home/config`.  A
module reads its key with `config_value`, which fails loudly when the key is
absent: there is no silent default.

| config key | question | variants | module |
|---|---|---|---|
| `rooms.unknown-parent` | new (a parent id not held here; read only in that case) | `refuse`, `keep` | 101 |
| `rooms.slash-in-name` | new (a room name holding `/`; read only in that case) | `refuse`, `allow` | 101 |
| (not switchable) | new: a bell listener fails after a room was written | `keep-and-raise` (built), `undo-and-raise` (not built) | 101 |
| (not switchable) | new, waits on state: room files surviving a power cut | `rename-only` (built), `fsync-then-rename` (not built) | 101 |
| `tree.parent-disclosure` | 3 | `always`, `shared-only` | 102 |
| — (not switchable) | new (does `where` keep only your own path, or also each recipient's view of it?) | `own-path` (built), `per-recipient` (not built) | 102 |
| — (not switchable; phase 2) | new (when a parent's list gains someone, are the child rooms re-announced so the reader's gap closes at once?) | `next-message` (what phase 1 models), `announce-on-add` (phase 2) | 102 |
| — (not switchable; phase 2) | new (taken off a parent's list, is a reader told to forget where the rooms below sit?) | `keep-what-was-told` (what phase 1 models), `forget-notice` (phase 2) | 102 |
| `recipients.split-shape` | removal | `fork`, `rekey` | 103 |
| `recipients.arrival-list` | 2 | `adder-only`, `heard-from`, `adder-list` | 103 |
| `recipients.split-parent` | new (split room's place in the tree) | `keep`, `top` | 103 |
| `recipients.split-alone` | new (split of a room of two leaves nobody) | `keep-empty`, `record-only` | 103 |
| `people.link-collision` | new | `suffix-id`, `numbered`, `id-folder` | 104 |
| `people.own-devices` | new (do own devices get a `people/` folder) | `none`, `all-tops` | 104 |
| `people.split-records` | new (is a split record still a shared room in `people/`) | `show`, `hide` | 104 |
| `people.slash-in-link-name` | new (a `/` in a room name, as a link name; read only in that case) | `percent`, `division-slash` | 104 |
| `log.record-shape` | 8 | `tsv`, `block`, `jsonl` | 105 |
| `log.edits` | 12 | `never`, `corrections`, `window` (+ `log.edit-window-seconds`) | 105 |
| `log.shape-mixing` | new (a log holding records of more than one shape after `log.record-shape` changes) | `per-record`, `fixed` | 105 |
| `log.duplicate-check` | new (issue 105: does the duplicate check need an index — a measurement; waits on state) | `remembered`, `scan` | 105 |
| (not switchable) | new (a deleted message: kept in the room view empty and marked, or vanishes) | `marked-empty` built, `vanish` not | 105 |
| (not switchable) | new (an edit arriving before its message; waits on phase 2) | `refuse` built | 105 |
| (not switchable) | new (a repeated id with different content: duplicate or error) | `duplicate` built | 105 |
| (not switchable; `input/phase-1-demo.config` overrides per key) | new (what the demo sets the choices a scene is not about to) | `first-listed` built, `builder-leaning` not (needs a machine-readable leaning field) | 106 |

A builder who finds another undecided choice adds a row here.

## The options tally

Each module keeps `<index>-options.lua` (the last index of its range: 019,
039, 059, 079, 099) returning a list of choices, one table per choice:

    {
      id          = "log-record-shape",       -- string, unique across modules
      question    = "...",                     -- plain English
      waits_on    = "owner" | "state",         -- who or what must resolve it
      detail      = "open question 8" | "issue 105: ...",
      config_key  = "log.record-shape",        -- or "" when not switchable
      variants    = { { name, summary, built_in, tests, findings }, ... },
      leaning     = "...",                     -- the builder's suggestion and why
    }

`waits_on = "state"` marks a choice blocked on something other than the
owner — another module's behaviour, a later phase, a measurement.  The
`scripts/tally-options` tool (issue 106) reads all of these and walks through
them one at a time.  (106 keeps its own at `src/phase-1-demo/119-options.lua`.)

What the tally accepts, exactly: files named `src/<module>/<3 digits>-options.lua`,
one folder deep (never inside `tests/`); `built_in`, `tests` and `findings`
may each be one string or a list of strings; every text field but
`config_key` must be non-empty; `variants` must be a non-empty list; an `id`
used twice anywhere is an error.

The phase 1 demo reads these files too: for every choice with a
`config_key`, the **first variant listed** is the value its homes start with
(unless `input/phase-1-demo.config` names another built variant), and the
scenes run every listed variant of the key they are about.  So the order of
`variants` matters, and a key the modules read must appear in some options
file or the demo cannot set it.

## The builders' board

`tmp/shared-memory/agent-network/` holds one append-only file per builder
(`101.log` … `106.log`).  A builder appends a dated line when an interface
becomes usable, when it changes something here, or when it has a question for
another builder; builders read each other's files while they work.
